{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>If your brother, your mother's son, entices you.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): even your brother, your mother's son, who was with you in one womb." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> our Rabbis of blessed memory (said) — even the son of his mother, who does not take a share with him in inheritance and does not hate him, he must not listen to him in this matter; no need to say the son of his father, who takes a share with him in inheritance and who perhaps hates him and gives him evil counsel." "<b>Whom you have not known, you and your fathers.</b> Rashi of blessed memory explained: see how despicable his counsel is, for he tells you to abandon what your fathers handed down to you." "<b>And it is fitting</b> to interpret that there is alluded here the two parts of faith, two parts: one from the side of tradition (kabbalah) and the second from the side of knowledge and wisdom. One who believes from the side of tradition because he received it thus from his fathers — that is a good faith, since the tradition is true, but it has no perfection because there is no wisdom in it. And behold, his imagination is like a blind man walking in the company of blind men, each one leaning his hand on his fellow, and there is one sighted man at the head of all of them who guides them — surely they all walk on a paved and straight path, since they follow the way of the sighted man. So too our faith from the side of tradition without wisdom is a paved path — the supporters and dependents lean one upon another up to Moses our master, peace be upon him, and we hold to his way, for with our own ears we heard and our fathers recounted to us. But there is no perfection in this faith, for one who believes from the side of tradition, if he hears the words of the strayers and the heretics, idol worshippers, he is not secure within himself that his heart will not turn to one of them, since he has no wisdom; but surely it is possible that his heart will turn — either he will believe or he will doubt, like the blind man who is not secure within himself that he will not stumble. But the sighted man is secure that he will not stumble, for the power of sight is strong with him and he needs no other. So too this believer is complete in himself, secure that his heart will not turn after those who alarm — and through his wisdom and knowledge he will refute their opinion and his heart will remain firm in his faith. Therefore a person needs that the matter of his faith from the side of tradition alone not suffice him, but rather he must study and grow wise until his faith is perfected and he has faith both from the side of tradition and from the side of knowledge. And concerning this our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'Be diligent in studying Torah, and know what to answer a heretic.' And this is what is mentioned here, the two parts of faith, and he said: 'Whom you have not known' — this is faith from the side of knowledge; 'you and your fathers' — this is the faith from the side of the tradition of the fathers."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:09.090346661Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}